\id 1JN Transparent Bible (working title) - pilot draft
\h 1 John
\toc1 The First Epistle General of John
\toc2 1 John
\toc3 1 Jn
\mt1 1 John
\c 5
\s1 Faith That Overcomes the World
\p
\v 1 Everyone who believes that Jesus is the Christ has been born of God, and everyone who loves him who begot also loves him who has been begotten of him.
\v 2 By this we know that we love the children of God, when we love God and keep his commandments.\f + \fr 5:2 \ft Text: TR \fqa keep\ft ; Maj., CT \fqa do\ft .\f*
\v 3 For this is the love of God, that we keep his commandments. And his commandments are not burdensome.
\v 4 For whatever has been born of God overcomes the world. And this is the victory that has overcome the world: our faith.
\v 5 Who is he who overcomes the world, but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?
\s1 The Testimony About the Son
\p
\v 6 This is he who came by water and blood, Jesus the Christ; not by water only, but by water and blood. And it is the Spirit who bears witness, because the Spirit is the truth.\f + \fr 5:6 \ft Text: TR \fqa Jesus the Christ\ft ; Maj., CT \fqa Jesus Christ \ft (KJV).\f*
\v 7 For there are three that bear witness in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit; and these three are one.\f + \fr 5:7 \ft Text: TR includes \fqa in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit; and these three are one. And there are three that bear witness on earth \ft (vv. 7–8, the “Johannine Comma”). Maj. and CT omit it and read: \fqa For there are three that bear witness: the Spirit and the water and the blood; and these three agree in one\ft . The words are found in Latin writers from about the fourth century onward and in many later Latin manuscripts. In Greek they are found in the text of only a handful of manuscripts, the earliest from the fourteenth century, and in the margins of a few others; no Greek Church Father quotes them. Erasmus added them to his Greek New Testament in his third edition (1522), and they were then printed in the TR editions behind the KJV.\f*
\v 8 And there are three that bear witness on earth: the Spirit, and the water, and the blood; and these three agree in one.
\v 9 If we receive the testimony of men, the testimony of God is greater; for this is the testimony of God which he has testified concerning his Son.
\v 10 Whoever believes in the Son of God has the testimony in himself. Whoever does not believe God has made him a liar, because he has not believed the testimony that God has given concerning his Son.
\v 11 And this is the testimony: that God has given us eternal life, and this life is in his Son.
\v 12 Whoever has the Son has life; whoever does not have the Son of God does not have life.
\s1 Confidence in Prayer
\p
\v 13 These things I have written to \zpl you\zpl* who believe in the name of the Son of God, that \zpl you\zpl* may know that \zpl you\zpl* have eternal life, and that \zpl you\zpl* may believe in the name of the Son of God.\f + \fr 5:13 \ft Text: TR, Maj. as in the text; CT \fqa I have written these things to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life\ft .\f*
\v 14 And this is the confidence that we have toward him, that if we ask anything according to his will, he hears us.
\v 15 And if we know that he hears us, whatever we ask, we know that we have the requests that we have asked of him.
\v 16 If anyone sees his brother sinning a sin not leading to death, he shall ask, and he will give him life for those who sin not leading to death.\f + \fr 5:16 \ft Note: “He” may be God, or the one who prays, as the means by which life is given.\f* There is a sin leading to death; I do not say that he should pray about that.\f + \fr 5:16 \ft Lit.: \fqa not toward death\ft ; KJV \fqa not unto death\ft . Also in v. 17.\f*
\v 17 All unrighteousness is sin, and there is a sin not leading to death.
\p
\v 18 We know that whoever has been born of God does not sin; but he who has been born of God keeps himself, and the wicked one does not touch him.\f + \fr 5:18 \ft Text: TR, Maj., NA28 \fqa himself\ft ; SBL, WH \fqa him\ft , which makes “he who has been born of God” refer to Christ.\f*
\v 19 We know that we are of God, and the whole world lies in the wicked one.\f + \fr 5:19 \ft Or: \fqa in wickedness \ft (KJV). Gk. \fqa en tō ponērō \ft can be masculine (“the wicked one”, as in v. 18) or neuter.\f*
\v 20 And we know that the Son of God has come and has given us understanding, that we may know him who is true; and we are in him who is true, in his Son Jesus Christ. This is the true God and eternal life.
\v 21 Little children, keep \zpl yourselves\zpl* from idols. Amen.\f + \fr 5:21 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa Amen\ft ; CT omits.\f*
